  <meta property="og:title" content="How to Remove Author Name from a PDF — Every Method (2026)" />
  <meta property="og:description" content="Your name is hidden inside every PDF you export. Here is how to remove the Author field permanently, on any device, for free." />
  <meta property="og:url" content="https://fileintel.me/blog/remove-author-from-pdf" />
  <meta property="og:type" content="article" />
  <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />

  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    "headline": "How to Remove Author Name from a PDF (2026) — Every Method",
    "datePublished": "2026-07-26",
    "dateModified": "2026-07-26",
    "author": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "FileIntel"},
    "publisher": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "FileIntel", "url": "https://fileintel.me"},
    "description": "A complete guide to removing the Author field from PDF files — browser-based tools, ExifTool command line, Adobe Acrobat, and Microsoft Word — with verification steps and compliance context.",
    "url": "https://fileintel.me/blog/remove-author-from-pdf",
    "mainEntityOfPage": "https://fileintel.me/blog/remove-author-from-pdf"
  }
  </script>

  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How do I remove the author name from a PDF?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The fastest method is FileIntel's PDF Metadata Remover at fileintel.me/pdf-metadata-remover — drop your PDF in, click Strip Metadata, download the clean file. It removes the Author field from both the Info dictionary and the XMP stream in under 5 seconds. For command line: exiftool -Author= document.pdf removes just the Author field. exiftool -all= document.pdf removes all metadata."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Where does the author name in a PDF come from?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The Author field is automatically populated by your operating system or Office account when you create or export a document. On Windows it comes from your Windows username or Microsoft 365 account name. On macOS it comes from your Apple ID or macOS account. On Google Docs it comes from your Google account display name. You never explicitly typed your name into the PDF — the software did it silently."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can I change the author name in a PDF instead of removing it?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes. In Acrobat Pro: File > Properties > Description > Author field — you can type a new value. With ExifTool: exiftool -Author='New Name' document.pdf. However, simply changing it in Acrobat only updates the Info dictionary — the XMP stream may still contain the original name. For a complete change, use ExifTool which updates both stores simultaneously."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does changing your Windows username change the author in future PDFs?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Partially. The Author field in Word-generated PDFs comes from the name set in Word's Options > General > Personalize, not your Windows username. Changing your Windows username does not change this. You need to update it in Word specifically: File > Options > General > 'User name' field. This affects future documents but not already-created files."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does removing the author from a PDF affect the document's appearance?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "No. The Author field is hidden metadata — it never appears on the visible pages of the document. Removing it has zero effect on the document's layout, content, fonts, or any visible element. The file size may decrease very slightly but the document looks identical before and after."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Is the author name stored in both the Info dictionary and the XMP stream?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes. Modern PDFs store the Author name in two places: the standard Info dictionary (readable via Acrobat's Properties panel) and the XMP stream (an XML block inside the PDF, readable via ExifTool as dc:creator or xmp:Author). Many tools only clear the Info dictionary. If you only see the Author field disappear in Acrobat Properties but it still shows in ExifTool, the XMP stream was not cleared."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Why does Word keep adding my name to PDFs even after I change it once?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Word stores your Author name in two places: the document's built-in properties (which applies to that specific file) and your Word user settings (which applies to all future documents). If you only changed it in the document properties, new documents still use the name from your Word user settings. To fix it permanently: File > Options > General > change the 'User name' field. Then clear the Author from any existing documents you want to send."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>

